:I got an Alphaserver 1000A 4/200 (32MB RAM). Sadly, this machine has a
:problem: after pressing the power button the system switches on and after
:2sec it switches off again. Any ideas why it does this? I noticed that one
:of the two fans (the one that cools the expansion cards) is missing. Can
:this be the reason? The manual says that if there is a problem with the fans
:the system switches off after ~10s, but mine does that after only 2secs. Any
:ideas?

  The system and the power supply do attempt to prevent damage to the
  system, and running with a failed or missing fan can obviously cause
  permanent damage.  

  While I don't know that it is the missing fan that is causing this
  particular power-down, I would immediately replace the (missing) fan
  and would try again.  

  I would not recommend running an Alpha or any other similar hardware
  without its complement of fans and its covers and shields in place.
  Thermal failures can be immediate, or they can manifest themselves
  over days or months, through vastly reduced disk drive and component
  electronics lifetimes.  Thermal-related failures can also lead to
  system instabilities, even if no physical damage is done.

  32MB of physical memory is well below the minimum requirements for any
  recent version OpenVMS Alpha I am familiar with.  I'd generally not try
  to run an Alpha system with less than 128 MB configured, and I'd tend
  to recommend configuring 192MB -- or more.


