In article <3FA28779.9010908@Flying-Disk.com>, Alan Frisbie <Usenet01REMOVE@Flying-Disk.com> writes:
:Bart Zorn wrote:
:> I am not sure what type of memory you need exactly, but as far as I 
:> know, 1000A's do not need 5 modules per bank, but only 4 (the 1000 does 
:> need 5!). The sockets for the 5th module are present but not used. My 
:> 1000A 5/300 has 4 * 4 * 32 MB and works fine. The modules are the same 
:> as being used in the VAX 4000/108.
:
:Wrong (at least partly).   The later/faster 1000A systems (like
:yours) only needed four SIMMs, but the older ones (4/266, for
:example) did need all five.   I think the dividing line was EV4
:to EV5, but I am not sure.

  Correct.

  Both the AlphaServer 1000 and the AlphaServer 1000A series EV4-class
  processors provide four banks of five SIMMs each.  Four SIMMs in the
  bank provide data storage, and the fifth provides the ECC coverage.

  AlphaServer 1000 and AlphaServer 1000A EV5-class processors provide
  four banks of four SIMMs each.  SIMMs are organized in in groups of
  four adjacent slots.  Bank 0 must be filled, and filled first.  The
  ECC bank is not used, as the EV5 processor and the required SIMMs
  provide the ECC coverage.

-- 

