Another suggestion: go to the PDP Unix Preservation Society site
(http://minnie.tuhs.org/PUPS/), find the components of VTserver that allow
you to copy images to a PDP-11-supported disk from a PC on the other end of
a serial line, and choose one of the many appropriate operating systems
available on the PUPS site (I'm running Unix 6th Ed. on my 11/34a).  It's
slow but effective - I think it took me a couple of hours to move the Unix
image to an RK05, but it boots just fine.

You won't find RSX-11 there, because that OS's current owners, Mentec,
haven't been willing to do the legwork (legal-wise) to offer a hobbyist
license.  (DEC's licenses were quite an artifact of the time: they licensed
a particular entity to run a particular bit of software on a particular bit
of hardware!  All the recent flap about Microsoft's practices seem pretty
silly when you consider the 'old' licensing models....)

DON'T toss that tape!  Regardless of the legal issues, it's still a
delightful artifact of the times.  I have an RK05 distribution of RSX-11 3.0
that I can't legally do anything with, but I retain it because of its
historical significance.  Of course, if you're not using it for commercial
gain, it's hard to see why Mentec would cause you any trouble - but far be
it from me to advise you to violate the strict interpretation of copyright
law.  (On one hand, I work in the software trade, and really hope that
people respect software companies' intellectual property rights; on the
other hand, there's no way we're making any money off long-discontinued
versions of products, and I'd be delighted if someone cared enough about
them to run them.  But I'm not one of my company's lawyers....)  So, even if
you're being a Very Good Boy regarding software licenses, there will likely
be a day when (a) Mentec "officially" doesn't care anymore, (b) Mentec
issues a hobbyist license, or (c) Mentec goes away and doesn't transfer ITS
license to someone who makes an issue of non-commercial, historical use.  Or
you can close your blinds and fire it up - as long as it doesn't get to 451
degrees F.  :-)

Good luck!  -- Ian

