image on the media, but I can't even remember finding it
there - and a search of the distribution media along with
a DUMP was always done past the files specified in the
RT-11 directory.  BINCOM was always used to check
the distribution media right to the end to look for any blocks
that had non-zero information present.

I am not saying TECO.SAV was NOT there, only that I
NEVER found it.  And I looked at every non-zero block
on every RL02 distribution media I ever found, also the
RK05 media.

In addition, some RT-11 users do not realize that RT-11
can be run from an RL02 and from an RK05 that is WRITE
PROTECTED.  RX50 and RX33 can also qualify, BUT
both RX01 and RX02 floppy drives from DEC can't even
notice the WRITE  PROTECT tab on those media - although
most 3rd party RX01 / RX02 compatible hardware could
do that.  In general, when I received any DEC distribution
media, a backup copy of the media was made BEFORE
anything else was done and the original NEVER used except
for making another backup.  If the user did boot an RL02
media, at the very least, the IND.SAV file was usually changed.
That is one reason why it is so VERY difficult to find a
"pristine distribution" any more.

In any case, there are a number of sites which carry all of
V4.00, V5.00 and V5.03 of RT-11.  If you can't find them,
please ask!

