V5.04A,  V5.04B,  V5.04C,  V5.04F
(especially on an RL02 media or the image) would be greatly
appreciated.

By the way, my definition of a "pristine distribution" is:

(a)  SWAP.SYS is ALL zeros

(b)  Neither LD.SYS nor LDX.SYS have any unit numbers
      in the file - i.e. they are exact copies of the assemble / link
     process after a SYSGEN

(c) IND.SAV is an exact copy after the assemble / link process
     as produced during a System Build by DEC

(d) The correct Volume ID / Owner is present in block 1 and
      no other junk is in blocks 0 to 67 - i.e. the media was all
      zeros and was a perfect media without any bad blocks
      before the files were copied to the media.

(e)  The bad block information in block 1 for an RL02 image
      is the data that would be placed there by that version of
      RT-11

(f)  For V5.0x distributions on an RL02, the boot monitor is
     RT11AI.SYS as set up by the command:
     COPY/BOOT  DL0:RT11AI.SYS  DL0:

Most of the above 6 points are obvious, but are often ignored
by users who are not as meticulous as they might be when a
duplicate is being made of a DEC distribution pack.  There is
also one advantage to making sure that all blocks on the media
are all zeros before the files are transferred.  When a DSK file
copy is made of any distribution, the blocks which are ALL
zeros AFTER the last file on the RL02 image need NOT be
retained.  Since each full RL02 image is 20450 blocks, only
three full RL02 images can fit on one RT-11 partition of
65535 blocks.  But most RT-11 distributions are only about
6000 blocks which means that ALL of the files and other
non-zero data can fit into a DSK file along with up to a dozen
other DSK files on just one RT-11 partition.  A copy back to
an actual RL02 is still the same RT-11 command:
COPY/DEVICE/FILES  dev:foobar.DSK  DL0:
although I also prefer to zero out the rest of the RL02 before
anything is copied.

Note that a real hardware RL02 can have bad blocks
which means that a user with real hardware must be very
careful.  Fortunately, when SIMH is used, that aspect is
NEVER (grin) a problem and the large hard drives that
are available these days mean that a few extra 10 MByte
RL02 container files (or even few hundred - or even a few
thousand if needed) are now easily available.

