"Douglas A. Gwyn" <DAGwyn@null.net> wrote in message news:<45Gcndh33_YS4x6iU-KYvQ@comcast.com>...
> > ... I certainly will avoid any discussion on a
> > point as off-topic as the number of tape marks between files.
> 
> I thought DEC used ANSI-standard magtape labels.

For suitable interpretations of "ANSI-standard" :-)  For a long time
RT-11 ANSI tapes were not readable by VMS's ANSI support, for example.

RT-11 5.4 and earlier wrote 512-byte headers, VMS was expecting 80-byte
headers.  If you read the ANSI spec carefully you see that it doesn't
actually specify the length of the header, it only tells you what's supposed
to be in the first 80 bytes.

Note that ANSI tapes can have double tape marks before logical EOT but not
in this context.

Tim.


